@meta
id: 27-14
title: A standing instruction
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: autocmd, argdo
par: 59
minutes: 7
vscode: a format-on-save extension, configured in three places
open: orders-2026-07.csv orders-2026-08.csv orders-2026-09.csv

@brief
Three monthly exports from the orders table. Every one of them has a space
after each comma, which the importer will not accept.

You could fix each file with a substitute and write it. Three substitutes,
three writes, and the fourth export next month gets the same treatment.

Instead, tell vim to do it on the way out. An autocommand is a standing
instruction with three parts — an event, a file pattern, and a command:

    :autocmd BufWritePre *.csv %s/, /,/ge

"Just before any `.csv` buffer is written, run that substitute on it." The
buffer is fixed in memory a moment before it hits the disk, and it applies
to every CSV you write for the rest of the session without you thinking
about it again.

Set it up and get all three files written. Only the first is loaded, so
something has to visit the other two.

@keys
| `:autocmd BufWritePre *.csv %s/, /,/ge` | run a command before every write |
| `:autocmd BufWritePost *` | after the write instead |
| `:autocmd BufRead,BufNewFile *.md setlocal tw=80` | two events, one command |
| `:argdo w` | run `:w` in every file in the arglist |
| `:autocmd BufWritePre` | list what is already registered for that event |

@hints
One autocommand, registered once, covers all three files. The work left
after that is getting vim to actually write each of them.
--
`:autocmd BufWritePre *.csv %s/, /,/ge` registers it. `:argdo w` walks
the arglist writing each file, which fires the event three times.
--
`:autocmd BufWritePre *.csv %s/, /,/ge` `Enter`, then `:argdo w` `Enter`,
then `:qa` `Enter`.

@solution
    :autocmd BufWritePre *.csv %s/, /,/ge<Enter>  :argdo w<Enter>  :qa<Enter>

Fifty-one keystrokes for three files, and the fifty-second file would be
free.

Read the parts:

    autocmd  BufWritePre  *.csv  %s/, /,/ge
             ^event       ^pat   ^command run in the buffer

**The event.** `:help autocmd-events` lists about a hundred. The ones you
will actually use: `BufRead` and `BufNewFile` (a file arrives),
`BufWritePre` and `BufWritePost` (a file leaves), `FileType` (vim decided
what it is), `InsertLeave`, `VimEnter`, `CursorHold`.

**The pattern.** Matched against the file name, with `*` wildcards. It is
not a directory glob unless you write one: `*.csv` matches any CSV
anywhere, `*/exports/*` matches by path.

**The command.** Any Ex command, run with that buffer current.

Two details that are not optional. The `e` flag on the substitute means
"no error if there is nothing to match" — without it, a clean CSV makes
the write fail with `E486` and you cannot save the file. Every autocommand
substitute you ever write wants that `e`. And `BufWritePre` changes the
buffer, so your undo history now has the reformatting in it: `u` after a
save undoes the autocommand's work, not yours.

`:argdo w` is doing the rounds here because `:wqa` only writes buffers that
have been loaded, and vim loads only the first file at startup.

@start orders-2026-07.csv
order_id, status, total_pence
01H8XA, CANCELLED, 4500
01H8XB, SHIPPED, 12000

@expected orders-2026-07.csv
order_id,status,total_pence
01H8XA,CANCELLED,4500
01H8XB,SHIPPED,12000

@start orders-2026-08.csv
order_id, status, total_pence
01H9QC, PENDING, 2300
01H9QD, CANCELLED, 8900

@expected orders-2026-08.csv
order_id,status,total_pence
01H9QC,PENDING,2300
01H9QD,CANCELLED,8900

@start orders-2026-09.csv
order_id, status, total_pence
01HAZE, SHIPPED, 15750
01HAZF, CANCELLED, 600

@expected orders-2026-09.csv
order_id,status,total_pence
01HAZE,SHIPPED,15750
01HAZF,CANCELLED,600
